module lexer
  source: ["lexer.ly"]
  why: "Tokenizer for Lyric source. Produces a Token stream classified by the TokenKind enum. The Lexer is a stateful scanner tracking line/column/position so every Token carries an accurate Span for downstream error reporting and source mapping."

  doc "Architecture":
    """
    Linear scan: raw text → Token stream. The Lexer walks the source
    byte-by-byte producing Token values with kind, text, and span.

    Keyword recognition uses a Dict<Sym, TokenKind> built once at
    construction and consulted by scan_ident. Hard keywords (class, func,
    struct, …) lex to their specific TokenKind. Contextual keywords (doc,
    why, source, field, lock, implements) are emitted as LIdent and
    resolved by the parser based on position.

    LexerState (save_state / restore_state) snapshots pos/line/column,
    bracket_depth, and any peeked token, enabling parser backtracking for
    ambiguous constructs such as struct literals vs blocks. The snapshot
    is a simple value copy — cheap and side-effect free.

    Bracket tracking: bracket_depth counts open () and [] only. Newlines
    inside brackets are suppressed (no SNewline emitted) so that
    multi-line argument lists / list literals don't terminate statements.
    Braces {} delimit blocks and do NOT participate in bracket_depth.

    String forms: scan_string, scan_triple_string, scan_fstring, and
    scan_char_lit handle the four literal shapes. Backtick-enclosed
    identifiers (`name`) are lexed as LBacktickSym by scan_backtick_sym,
    which the parser desugars into symbol literals. F-strings encode
    escaped braces {{ and }} as sentinel bytes \\x01 and \\x02 in the
    token text so the parser can later distinguish them from interpolation
    boundaries; the parser is responsible for decoding the sentinels.
    Triple-quoted strings (\"\"\"...\"\"\") automatically trim leading and
    trailing whitespace-only lines. Numeric literals support underscores
    as digit separators for readability.

    Comments are NOT emitted as tokens. The scan loop consumes // and
    /* */ comments and discards them, keeping the token stream clean
    for parsing.

    Token splitting (e.g. >> → > >) for nested generics like Dict<Dict<V>>
    is explicitly NOT a lexer responsibility — the lexer emits OShr and
    the parser uses pushBack to split it when a closing > is expected.
    """

  doc "Technical Debt":
    """
    Unicode: the bootstrap lexer is ASCII-only. Full UTF-8 support is
    required for the production compiler to handle international
    characters in identifiers and strings.

    Block comments: /* */ support is defined in the formal model but
    not yet implemented in the source code.

    Error recovery: unknown characters currently fall back to being
    treated as identifiers. A dedicated SError token would improve
    robustness of parser error recovery.

    Static keyword table: the keyword Dict is re-initialized per Lexer
    instance. Moving it to a global constant (once the language supports
    static initialization of complex types) would eliminate redundant
    allocation.
    """

  invariant "Keyword Lookup":
    procedural
    """
    Keyword resolution MUST go through lookup_keyword, which consults
    the Lexer's keywords Dict<Sym, TokenKind> built once by
    init_keywords. Lookup is O(1) via Sym hashing.

    Hard keywords (KLyric, KFunc, KClass, KStruct, KEnum, KInterface,
    KImpl, KLet, KIf, KElse, KFor, KWhile, KMatch, KReturn, KBreak,
    KContinue, KTrue, KFalse, KNil, KSelf, KMut, …) always lex to
    their specific TokenKind.

    Contextual keywords — doc, why, source, fake, field, lock,
    implements — are emitted as LIdent. The parser resolves
    them positionally so they remain usable as ordinary identifiers
    in field/param/variable names.
    """

  invariant "Line Continuation After Binary Operators":
    procedural
    """
    The lexer suppresses newlines after binary operators. If the last
    consumed token was a binary operator (as identified by is_binary_op),
    the next SNewline is suppressed and the expression continues on the
    following line. This is tracked via last_kind on the Lexer, updated
    by next() on every consumed token.

    Binary operators for continuation: +, -, *, /, %, ==, !=, <=, >=,
    &&, ||, &, ^, <<, >>, =, +=, -=, *=, /=, <, >, comma, ->, =>.

    bracket_depth tracks () and [] nesting only. While bracket_depth
    > 0, newline bytes are consumed without emitting SNewline so that
    multi-line argument lists and list literals stay one statement.
    {} braces delimit blocks and MUST NOT increment bracket_depth.

    Both bracket_depth and last_kind are part of LexerState and MUST
    be saved/restored by save_state and restore_state so backtracking
    does not corrupt newline suppression.
    """

  invariant "Newline Collapsing":
    procedural
    """
    The lexer automatically collapses multiple consecutive newlines
    into a single SNewline token. After emitting one SNewline, any
    immediately following newline bytes are consumed without producing
    additional tokens. This simplifies the parser's job of handling
    statement boundaries — it never sees runs of empty-line separators.
    """

  invariant "F-String Sentinels":
    procedural
    """
    Inside an f-string, escaped braces {{ and }} MUST be stored in
    the token text as sentinel bytes \\x01 and \\x02 respectively.
    Real { and } in the token text continue to mark interpolation
    boundaries.

    The parser is responsible for decoding \\x01 → { and \\x02 → }
    when materializing the literal string segments of the f-string.
    The lexer MUST NOT emit { or } for escaped braces — that would
    be indistinguishable from interpolation.
    """

  invariant "Comment Handling":
    procedural
    """
    Comments are NOT emitted as tokens. Line comments (//) are scanned
    and collected into an ArrayList relation on the Lexer instance.
    Block comments (/* */) are consumed and discarded.

    Downstream consumers (parser, formatter) MUST NOT expect comment
    tokens in the token stream. The collected comments are available
    via the Lexer's side channel for tools such as documentation
    generators or code formatters that need to preserve them.
    """

  invariant "Token Splitting Boundary":
    procedural
    """
    Nested generics like Dict<Dict<V>> produce the source text >>,
    which the lexer MUST emit as a single OShr token. The lexer does
    NOT attempt to split OShr into two PGt tokens based on context.

    Splitting OShr → PGt PGt for closing nested generic argument
    lists is a parser-side responsibility, handled via a pushBack
    mechanism in the parser's expectation logic.
    """

  typedef TokenKind: enum { KLyric, KFunc, KClass, KStruct, KEnum, KInterface, KRelation, KField, KDestructor, KImport, KImplements, KImpl, KExtends, KAs, KIs, KType, KWhere, KOwns, KRefs, KMut, KSelf, KFrom, KTrue, KFalse, KNil, KPub, KLet, KIf, KElse, KFor, KIn, KWhile, KMatch, KReturn, KBreak, KContinue, KSpawn, KSelect, KCase, KLock, KYield, LIdent, LIntLit, LFloatLit, LStringLit, LTripleString, LFString, LCharLit, LBacktickSym, PLParen, PRParen, PLBrace, PRBrace, PLBracket, PRBracket, PComma, PColon, PDot, PArrow, PFatArrow, PBiArrow, PPipe, PQuestion, PLt, PGt, OAssign, OPlus, OMinus, OStar, OSlash, OPercent, OEqEq, OBangEq, OLtEq, OGtEq, OAmpAmp, OPipePipe, OBang, OAmp, OCaret, OShl, OShr, OPlusEq, OMinusEq, OStarEq, OSlashEq, SNewline, SEOF }
    source: lexer.ly:15

  class Token
    source: lexer.ly:39
    field kind: TokenKind
      doc: "Discriminator selecting which lexical category this token belongs to (keyword, literal, punctuation, operator, separator). Drives all parser dispatch."
    field span: Span
      doc: "Source location (file, start, end) anchoring this token to the original text. Enables precise error reporting and source mapping through all compiler stages."
    field text: string
      doc: "Raw source text captured by this token. For string literals this includes escape sequences as-is; for f-strings it contains sentinel bytes (\\x01/\\x02) encoding escaped braces."

  func init_keywords() -> Dict<Sym, TokenKind>
    source: lexer.ly:47

  func lookup_keyword(lex: Lexer, text: string) -> TokenKind?
    source: lexer.ly:93

  struct LexerState
    source: lexer.ly:106
    field bracket_depth: i32
      doc: "Snapshot of open-paren/bracket nesting depth. Restored on backtrack so newline suppression inside () and [] stays consistent."
    field column: i32
      doc: "One-based column number at the time of the snapshot."
    field last_kind: TokenKind
      doc: "Kind of the last token consumed by next(). Used by scan() to suppress newlines after binary operators for line continuation."
    field line: i32
      doc: "One-based line number at the time of the snapshot."
    field peeked_token: Token?
      doc: "Buffered lookahead token, if any. Must be saved and restored so that peek()/next() ordering is preserved across backtracking."
    field pos: i32
      doc: "Byte offset into the source string at the time of the snapshot."

  class Lexer
    source: lexer.ly:115
    field bracket_depth: i32
      doc: "Counts open ( and [ tokens. While > 0, newlines are suppressed so multi-line argument lists and list literals stay one statement. Braces {} do NOT affect this counter."
    field column: i32
      doc: "One-based column tracker, advanced by advance() on every byte consumed."
    field filename: Sym?
      doc: "Interned filename attached to every Pos the lexer produces, threading through to Span and ultimately to error messages."
    field keywords: Dict<Sym, TokenKind>?
      doc: "Keyword table mapping Sym(identifier) → TokenKind for hard keywords. Built once by init_keywords and consulted by scan_ident. Optional because the field is wired up after construction; lookup_keyword must tolerate a missing table."
    field last_kind: TokenKind
      doc: "Kind of the last token consumed by next(). Updated by next() on every consumed token. Consulted by scan() at newline boundaries — if last_kind is a binary operator, the newline is suppressed for line continuation."
    field line: i32
      doc: "One-based line tracker, incremented whenever a newline byte is consumed."
    field peeked_token: Token?
      doc: "Buffered lookahead token produced by peek(). next() drains this before calling scan(), ensuring each token is scanned at most once."
    field pos: i32
      doc: "Current byte offset into src. Monotonically advances; never rewound except by restore_state."
    field src: string
      doc: "Complete source text being tokenized. The lexer indexes into this via pos; the string is never mutated."
    method save_state(self) -> LexerState
      source: lexer.ly:128
      why: "Captures a value-type snapshot of pos, line, column, bracket_depth, last_kind, and peeked_token. Used by the parser to checkpoint before speculative parses (e.g. disambiguating struct literals from blocks). The snapshot is a simple value copy — cheap and side-effect free."
    method restore_state(self, state: LexerState)
      source: lexer.ly:139
      why: "Reverts the lexer to a previously saved LexerState, ensuring that backtracking is side-effect free. All mutable fields — pos, line, column, bracket_depth, last_kind, peeked_token — are restored atomically."
    method current_pos(self) -> Pos
      source: lexer.ly:196
      why: "Returns the current source position as a Pos value. Used by the parser to anchor error messages and AST nodes to specific source locations."
    method advance(self) -> u8
      source: lexer.ly:200
    method peek_char(self) -> u8
      source: lexer.ly:215
    method peek_at(self, offset: i32) -> u8
      source: lexer.ly:222
    method peek(self) -> Token
      source: lexer.ly:231
      why: "Returns the next token without advancing the scanner. Caches the result in peeked_token so repeated peeks are O(1). The parser uses this for lookahead-driven dispatch without consuming the token."
    method next(self) -> Token
      source: lexer.ly:241
      why: "Consumes and returns the next token. Drains peeked_token first, otherwise calls scan(). Updates last_kind on every consumed token so that scan() can apply binary-operator line continuation on the following newline."
    method scan_escape(self, buf: StringBuilder) -> bool
      source: lexer.ly:256
    method scan_char_lit(self, start: Pos) -> Token
      source: lexer.ly:287
    method scan_backtick_sym(self, start: Pos) -> Token
      source: lexer.ly:303
      why: "Scans backtick-enclosed identifiers (`name`) and emits LBacktickSym. The parser desugars these into symbol literals."
    method scan_string(self, start: Pos) -> Token
      source: lexer.ly:320
    method scan_triple_string(self, start: Pos) -> Token
      source: lexer.ly:346
      why: "Scans triple-quoted multi-line raw text blocks (\"\"\"...\"\"\"). Leading and trailing whitespace-only lines are automatically trimmed from the captured text."
    method scan_fstring(self, start: Pos) -> Token
      source: lexer.ly:363
      why: "Scans f-string literals (f\"...\"). Encodes escaped braces {{ and }} as sentinel bytes \\x01 and \\x02 in the token text so the parser can distinguish literal braces from interpolation boundaries {expr}."
    method scan_number(self, start: Pos) -> Token
      source: lexer.ly:407
      why: "Scans integer and floating-point numeric literals. Underscores are allowed as digit separators for readability (e.g. 1_000_000)."
    method scan_ident(self, start: Pos) -> Token
      source: lexer.ly:437
      why: "Scans an identifier and consults the keyword lookup table. Hard keywords emit their specific TokenKind; all other identifiers (including contextual keywords like field, lock, implements) emit LIdent for parser-side resolution."
    method scan(self) -> Token
      source: lexer.ly:468
      why: "The main lexing loop: skips whitespace and comments, then dispatches on the next byte to the appropriate scan_* helper, producing exactly one Token per call. Honors bracket_depth to suppress newlines inside () and []. This is the single entry point feeding peek and next."

